.TH CCARCHIVE 1 "2026-10-05" "atelier instruments" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ccarchive \- durably preserve every Claude Code transcript, word for word
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ccarchive
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B ccarchive \-\-verify
.RI [ \-\-materialise ]
.RI [ \-\-dest
.IR dir ]
.br
.B ccarchive \-\-audit
.RI [ \-\-materialise ]
.RI [ \-\-dest
.IR dir ]
.br
.B ccarchive \-\-restore
.RI [ \-\-delta ]
.RI [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.RI [ \-\-force ]
.br
.B ccarchive \-\-rekey
.RI [ \-\-dest
.IR dir ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Claude Code records each session as a raw JSON-lines log under
.IR ~/.claude/projects/ <encoded-repo>/ <session-uuid> .jsonl
(plus subagent logs beneath it). That file is the complete, word-for-word
record of the session \(em every message, timestamp, model, token count, tool
call and thinking block. It is also
.BR ephemeral :
Claude Code deletes logs older than its
.I cleanupPeriodDays
setting (30 by default), so the history disappears on a rolling window.
.PP
.B ccarchive
mirrors every one of those logs \(em and the offloaded payloads, subagent
metadata, memory and history that go with them (see
.BR CAPTURE ) \(em
into a compressed, append-only archive that outlives that cleanup. Where
.BR cctranscript (1)
.I reads
the logs,
.B ccarchive
.I keeps
them.
.PP
The archive is an
.B incremental per-file gzip mirror :
each captured file
.IR <rel>/<name>
under the source becomes
.IR <dest>/<rel>/<name> .gz
under the archive. Only files that changed since the last run are
recompressed, so it is cheap to run often and light on a synced destination
such as iCloud Drive (only new or updated files upload). Each archived
session stays individually readable \(em decompress it and hand it to
.BR cctranscript (1),
or search the whole archive with
.BR zgrep (1).
.PP
The tool is
.B append-only by contract:
it never deletes from the archive. When Claude Code's cleanup removes a source
log, the archived copy remains \(em that is the whole point. It does not parse
the JSON, it preserves the bytes, so it is immune to any change in Claude
Code's internal log format.
.SH CAPTURE
What
.B ccarchive
captures is an
.B allowlist,
not "everything under the source root": each class below is preserved as raw
bytes; anything unrecognised is left out by default. This is stated here because
a silent exclusion is invisible \(em an archive that dropped referenced content
while advertising completeness would be dishonest about what it holds.
.PP
.B Captured.
.TP
.I <encoded-repo>/<uuid>.jsonl
Session transcripts, and nested subagent logs beneath the session
.RI ( <uuid>/subagents/*.jsonl ) \(em
the core record, at any depth.
.TP
.I <uuid>/subagents/*.meta.json
.B Subagent metadata sidecars.
Claude Code writes one small JSON file beside each subagent log recording how
that agent was spawned: its type and description, the
.I tool_use
id of the block that spawned it, its spawn depth and parent agent, the model it
ran on, and any worktree it was given. None of that is recoverable from the
transcripts alone \(em the
.I tool_use
id is the only join back to the spawning block in the parent session log, and
the depth and parent are what disambiguate an agent that itself spawned agents
into the same directory. Matched by name
.RI ( *.meta.json )
but
.B scoped to a subagents/ directory,
so a
.I .meta.json
appearing anywhere else stays excluded by default like any other unrecognised
file, rather than being swept in sight-unseen.
.TP
.IR <uuid>/tool-results/* " and " <uuid>/toolu_*
.B Tool-result sidecars.
When a tool output is large, Claude Code offloads the payload to one of these
files and leaves only a pointer in the
.I .jsonl.
Capturing them closes a real hole: without them an archived transcript can
dangle a reference to content that was pruned from the live store and never
preserved. The modern shape is
.IR tool-results/ ;
.I toolu_*
is the legacy sibling. Both are opaque bytes \(em taken as-is, whatever the
extension.
.TP
.I <project>/memory/*.md
.B Per-project auto-memory
\(em the durable, cross-session working state Claude Code keeps for a project
(a memory index and its named entries). It is not a transcript, but it is part
of the working record and lives nowhere else.
.TP
.I ~/.claude/history.jsonl
.B The top-level prompt/command history.
This one file sits
.I beside
the projects directory, one level
.I above
the source root, so capturing it is a deliberate reach up out of
.IR ~/.claude/projects .
It is archived under a reserved
.I _external/
path that cannot collide with an encoded project directory, and
.B \-\-restore
returns it to its real home above the source root \(em never rewritten inside
.IR projects/ .
Override its location with
.B CCARCHIVE_HISTORY
(mainly for testing).
.PP
.B Not captured.
The following are deliberately excluded and, on a machine wipe, are not
recoverable from the archive:
.IR <project>/webfetch-*.pdf
(WebFetch page snapshots \(em re-fetchable, marginal),
.I ~/.claude/file-history/
(per-session edit checkpoints \(em large, and overlapping repository git
history),
.IR ~/.claude/sessions/ ", " ~/.claude/session-env/ " and " ~/.claude/shell-snapshots/
(ephemeral per-session runtime and shell state), and Claude Code's
.IR config ", " plugins " and " caches
.RI ( settings*.json ", " ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md ", " plugins/ ", " backups/ ", the various cache directories)."
These are runtime, re-derivable, or config that belongs to its own backup story,
not to a transcript archive.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-dest " dir"
Archive into
.I dir
instead of the default, which is a
.I cc-transcripts
folder in the operator's iCloud Drive. May also be set with the
.B CCARCHIVE_DEST
environment variable.
.TP
.BI \-\-source " dir"
Read transcripts from
.I dir
instead of
.IR ~/.claude/projects .
Mainly for testing.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Report what would be archived (or, with
.BR \-\-restore ,
restored) and write nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Overwrite an archived copy even when the source is smaller than the size
recorded at capture. Without it such a shrink is refused \(em see
.B INTEGRITY
below. With
.BR \-\-restore ,
it also overrides the refusal to overwrite a live file that is newer than the
archived copy, and the refusal to restore an entry that has no recorded sha256
\(em see
.B RESTORE
below. It never overrides a restore whose archived bytes fail their recorded
sha256.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-repo\-dest
Permit an archive directory that sits inside a git work tree. Refused by
default: the archive holds personal transcripts, and a repository destination
is one commit away from publishing them.
.TP
.B \-\-verify
Re-hash every archived transcript and compare it against the sha256 recorded
when it was archived, and check the manifest's own signature. A mirror that will
not decompress is reported by name; it does not stop the check. See
.B INTEGRITY
and
.B SIGNING
below.
.TP
.B \-\-rekey
Mint a fresh signing key (replacing any existing one) and re-sign the current
manifest with it. The deliberate key roll \(em after suspected key exposure, or
on a cadence. See
.B SIGNING
below.
.TP
.B \-\-audit
Compare the
.I live
store against the archive and report how it has drifted from what was
preserved \(em mutated, renamed, grown, new or pruned transcripts. Read-only.
See
.B AUDIT
below.
.TP
.B \-\-materialise
With
.B \-\-verify
or
.BR \-\-audit ,
read files whose local bytes iCloud has evicted (see
.B DATALESS
below) instead of skipping them. This deliberately
.I re-downloads
each evicted file to hash it, so it is opt-in \(em the default skips them to
keep a routine check from dragging the whole archive back onto disk. No effect
on any other mode.
.TP
.B \-\-restore
Restore transcripts from the archive back into the live store: gunzip each
.IR <dest>/<rel> .jsonl.gz
to
.IR <source>/<rel> .jsonl.
By default every archived transcript is a target (rebuild a lost store); with
.B \-\-delta
only the drift
.BR \-\-audit
reports is. Writes only under the source. See
.B RESTORE
below.
.TP
.B \-\-delta
With
.BR \-\-restore ,
restore only what
.B \-\-audit
flags as drift \(em the
.BR mutated ,
.B pruned
and
.B renamed
buckets \(em rather than the whole archive. Ignored without
.BR \-\-restore .
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit a machine-readable JSON summary instead of the human report. Never
styled; safe to pipe into
.BR jq (1).
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a concise usage summary and exit. This manual is the full reference.
.SH RUNNING IT
.B ccarchive
is run by hand. It installs nothing and schedules nothing: no launchd agent, no
cron line, no login item. An archive is only as current as its last run, so run
it more often than Claude Code's
.I cleanupPeriodDays
(30 by default): a session whose source is pruned before the first run that sees
it is not captured. Running it often is cheap, because only new or changed files
are written.
.PP
Earlier versions could install a launchd agent. This version neither installs
nor
manages one, and refuses the old
.BR \-\-install\-schedule ,
.B \-\-schedule\-status
and
.B \-\-uninstall\-schedule
flags by name (exit status 2) rather than ignore them. To remove an agent an
older version left behind, unload and delete it yourself:
.B launchctl unload \-w ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.ccarchive.archive.plist
and then delete that file.
.SH INTEGRITY
gzip carries a CRC-32, so a corrupted
.I .gz
fails to decompress \(em but that is weak and only proves the file is
self-consistent, not that it still equals what was captured. So when it
archives a transcript,
.B ccarchive
records a sha256 of the transcript's raw bytes in
.IR <dest>/manifest.json .
.PP
.B ccarchive \-\-verify
re-hashes every archived
.I .gz
and compares it against the manifest, reporting any
.B mismatch
(mutation, or a bit flip that still decompresses),
.B missing
file, or
.B corrupt
file (one that will not decompress at all: bit rot, a truncated copy, a file
that
is not gzip), and exiting non-zero if the archive does not verify. A corrupt or
otherwise unreadable mirror is named and the check carries on with the next
file;
it never ends the verify. The manifest tracks
the
.I archive
(append-only), not the live sources: a session pruned by Claude Code's cleanup
keeps its recorded hash because its
.I .gz
is kept.
.PP
A file present in the archive but absent from the manifest is reported
.B UNMANIFESTED
and fails the verify: it is either injected, or history the manifest lost, and
both deserve a human eye. Entries whose source was pruned before hashing
existed are marked
.B fromArchive
in the manifest \(em their hash attests the archived copy only, not the
original raw bytes \(em and
.B \-\-verify
counts them distinctly.
.PP
The archive never
.I deletes
\(em but an ordinary run does
.I overwrite
an archived copy when the source is newer. Because sessions only ever grow, a
source that is
.I smaller
than the size recorded at capture is treated as suspect (truncation, disk
fault, sync accident) and the overwrite is refused with a non-zero exit;
.B \-\-force
is the deliberate override. The mirror image \(em a source restored from
backup with an
.I older
modification time \(em is silently left un-archived, since the archived copy
is newer.
.PP
The guard is size-only and applies to every captured class alike. Two of those
classes are whole documents rather than append-only logs \(em a memory
.I .md
and a subagent
.I .meta.json
\(em so a legitimate rewrite of one can be smaller than what was recorded and
will be refused until
.B \-\-force
is given. That is safe-over-silent by choice, and it costs little: the guard can
only fire on a source that is both
.I newer
than its mirror and
.I smaller
than the recorded size, i.e. on a rewrite, never on a first capture.
.PP
The shrink guard's sibling is a
.B layout-drift alarm :
a run that finds
.I zero
transcripts under the source while the manifest already records some exits
non-zero instead of reporting success \(em the live directory moved or Claude
Code changed where it writes logs, and a silently empty mirror would stop
growing while looking healthy.
.PP
The sha256 manifest defends against
.I accidental
corruption. On its own it is not tamper-proof \(em an adversary who rewrote a
.I .gz
.I and
the manifest would pass. That gap is closed by
.BR SIGNING ,
below: the manifest is signed with a key kept off the archive, so a forged
manifest fails
.BR \-\-verify .
.SH SIGNING
The manifest is the trust anchor, but a manifest an attacker can rewrite anchors
nothing: with write access to the archive volume (for the default iCloud Drive
destination, anyone or anything that can write there) a tamperer could rewrite a
.I .gz
.I and
recompute the manifest hash, and a hash-only
.B \-\-verify
would pass. So
.B ccarchive
.B signs
the manifest.
.PP
When it writes the manifest, it computes an
.B HMAC\-SHA256
over the manifest's exact bytes using a secret key held
.I off
the archive volume, and writes a detached signature beside it as
.IR manifest.json.sig
(algorithm, a non-secret key fingerprint, and the MAC).
.B \-\-verify
recomputes the MAC with the local key: a manifest altered after signing no
longer carries a MAC the key reproduces, so the tampering is
.BR "tamper-evident" .
Every manifest write re-signs; a pre-signing archive is migrated to a signed one
on the next ordinary run.
.PP
The manifest is saved, and re-signed, every 50 mirrors written and again if a
run fails part-way, so a run that dies leaves the manifest covering every mirror
it had written. A manifest entry is only ever set after its mirror is fully
written. An entry that already lags its mirror is not rewritten by an ordinary
run; it stays as recorded and
.B \-\-verify
reports it. Overlapping runs are not locked against.
.PP
The scheme is symmetric (HMAC), not a public-key signature, by design: the
signer and the verifier are the same machine and user, so a locally-held key is
the simplest fit. A keypair would buy something only if the archive had to be
verified somewhere the signing key must not go \(em not the case here, and a
public key
shipped
.I inside
the archive would just be replaced by the same write-capable tamperer.
.PP
.B The key.
The key is stored, hex-encoded and mode
.BR 0600 ,
at
.I ~/.claude/ccarchive-signing.key
by default \(em on local disk, beside Claude Code's own data, never synced into
the archive. Set
.B CCARCHIVE_KEYFILE
to override the path; a non-interactive run (from cron, say) reads it there
without a Keychain prompt (a file is chosen over the macOS Keychain precisely
because background contexts cannot reliably reach the Keychain, and this key
guards tamper-evidence, not a confidential value). It is
minted at first use with 32 bytes of entropy \(em never a hand-composed value.
.PP
.B Rotation.
The key is re-mintable at will:
.B \-\-rekey
generates a fresh one and re-signs the current manifest, which is all a roll
needs \(em the signature only ever attests the
.I current
manifest, so nothing is lost. Roll on any credible suspicion of key exposure,
and on a cadence besides. But because the key is what a
.I new
machine needs to verify existing signatures, keep an out-of-band backup of it: a
lost key cannot be re-minted to a value that verifies old signatures (the same
redundancy obligation atelier's secrets doctrine places on a store's master
key).
.PP
.B Failure semantics.
.B \-\-verify
never reports an unverified manifest as clean. It distinguishes, and exits
non-zero for, each non-green case: a
.B MISMATCH
(the manifest changed after signing \(em tamper evident); a manifest signed by a
.B different key
(an unexpected roll, or a foreign signer); an
.B unsigned
manifest (a pre-signing archive to migrate, or a removed signature); and an
.B unverifiable
manifest \(em no key on this machine, so the signature cannot be checked at all
(bring the key out-of-band, or establish one with a run). Only a manifest whose
signature the local key reproduces exits
.BR 0 .
The signature is
.I tamper-evident,
not tamper-proof: it detects a forgery at verify time, it does not prevent the
write, and an attacker who also steals the key can forge a valid signature \(em
which is why the key's confidentiality and cheap rotation matter.
.SH AUDIT
.B \-\-verify
asks one question \(em is the
.I archive
still intact against its own manifest?
.B \-\-audit
asks the other \(em has the
.I live
store drifted from what was archived? It hashes every live transcript and
places it in one bucket:
.PP
.B synced
(live bytes match the recorded sha256; the healthy majority),
.B grown
(the archived bytes are a strict prefix of the live file \(em a plain append
since the last run, which the next
.B ccarchive
will capture),
.B mutated
(the live file diverges from the archived copy by something other than growth
\(em rewritten, or shrunk/truncated),
.B renamed
(a live file whose content matches an archived transcript recorded under a
different path that is now gone from the live store),
.B new
(an unarchived live file matching nothing archived) and
.B pruned
(an archived transcript with no live counterpart \(em the expected steady
state once Claude Code's cleanup has run).
.PP
Only
.B mutated
and
.B renamed
are drift: the archive no longer equals the live session's history, or is
keyed by a stale path. Those exit non-zero and are listed by name;
.BR grown ,
.B new
and
.B pruned
are normal and only counted. The audit reads both trees and writes nothing.
.SH DATALESS
The default archive destination is iCloud Drive, and macOS's
.I Optimise Mac Storage
may evict the local bytes of an archived
.I .gz
it judges unused, leaving a
.B dataless
placeholder: the content is still in the cloud and faults back on the next read,
but no bytes sit on disk. Reading such a file re-downloads it. That is fine for
a deliberate
.BR \-\-restore ,
but a whole-archive
.B \-\-verify
that re-hashed every file would drag the entire history back onto disk \(em
costly, and it defeats the point of eviction.
.PP
So
.B ccarchive
detects eviction (the
.B SF_DATALESS
bit in the file's
.BR st_flags ,
read via
.BR stat (1)
\(em Node exposes no
.I st_flags
field of its own) and, by default,
.B \-\-verify
.I skips
an evicted file, reporting it
.B evicted
\(em a bucket distinct from
.B missing
and
.BR mismatch ,
and
.I not
a failure: the copy is intact in the cloud, merely not local, so it does not
exit non-zero.
.B \-\-audit
likewise leaves a changed live file whose archived copy is evicted
.I undetermined
rather than faulting the archive back to classify it. Pass
.B \-\-materialise
to read the evicted files anyway (re-downloading them).
.PP
A run never needs to read an evicted mirror's bytes. Mirrors are written by
creating a new file and renaming it over the old one (see
.BR "ATOMIC WRITES" ),
so replacing an evicted placeholder does not open it; an in-place rewrite is
what
used to fail on one with
.I Unknown system error \-11
(macOS's
.BR EDEADLK ,
returned when the system will not download an evicted file on demand). The one
read a run can still make of a mirror is the hash backfill of an archived file
that has no manifest entry. An evicted one is skipped there and reported as
.B not checked (offloaded),
and so is any read that fails with that error. Not checked is not a failure: it
exits
.BR 0 .
.PP
Detection is metadata-only \(em
.BR stat (1)
does
.I not
fault an evicted file back, and neither does the mtime check an ordinary run
uses \(em so archiving new sessions and writing the manifest never trigger a
bulk re-download. Off macOS, or on a non-iCloud volume, the bit is simply absent
and
the whole mechanism cleanly no-ops (nothing is ever mis-reported as evicted).
Because a fixture can never be genuinely evicted, this behaviour is exercised in
the tests through a simulation seam; the underlying
.B SF_DATALESS
read was verified read-only against a real evicted iCloud file.
.SH ATOMIC WRITES
Every file the tool writes into the archive \(em each mirror, the manifest and
its
signature \(em is written to a uniquely named temporary file in the same
directory
(\fI.<name>.<pid>.<random>.tmp\fR), flushed to disk, given its final
modification
time, and then renamed over the target. The existing file is never opened for
writing. So a run killed mid-write leaves the previous good copy in place
instead
of a truncated one that looks fresh, and a symbolic link planted at a mirror
path
is replaced, not followed: nothing is ever written through a link to somewhere
outside the archive. A symbolic link found
.I inside
the archive, in a directory on the way to a mirror, is refused: that mirror is
named in the report as not written, and the run fails at the end.
.PP
A temporary file left behind by a run that died is ignored by every walk,
.B \-\-verify
and
.BR \-\-audit ,
and is removed by the next ordinary run. A temporary file whose process is still
running belongs to another run and is left alone. Restore writes live files the
same way.
.SH ONE BAD FILE
A single bad file never aborts a run. A source file that cannot be read, a
source
directory that cannot be listed, or a source that vanishes between the walk and
its read is skipped and named in the report (the
.I unreadable
list under
.BR \-\-json ),
the rest of the run completes and the manifest is saved, and the run then exits
non-zero so it is not missed. A mirror that will not decompress is named in the
same way by
.BR \-\-verify ,
.B \-\-restore
and the run's hash backfill (the
.I corrupt
list), and processing continues. Anything that does stop a run \(em an
unreadable
manifest, a destination that cannot be written \(em ends in one line on standard
error, or a structured
.I {"error": ...}
object on standard output under
.BR \-\-json ,
and exit status 2; there is no stack trace.
.PP
Read-only modes (\fB\-\-verify\fR, \fB\-\-audit\fR, \fB\-\-restore\fR and any
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR) never rename or overwrite anything. If the manifest is not
valid
JSON they stop with an error and leave it where it is. Only an ordinary run sets
an invalid manifest aside, as
.IR manifest.json.corrupt\-<UTC\ timestamp> ,
never over an earlier set-aside copy, and then starts a new one. A manifest that
exists but cannot be read (an I/O error, or an evicted placeholder) is not
treated as invalid and is never set aside: the run stops and changes nothing.
.SH RESTORE
.B \-\-restore
is the inverse of archiving: it decompresses archived transcripts back into the
live store, gunzipping each
.IR <dest>/<rel> .jsonl.gz
to
.IR <source>/<rel> .jsonl.
It has two shapes. Plain
.B \-\-restore
targets every transcript in the manifest \(em the tool for rebuilding a live
store that was wiped or lost, or seeding a new machine from the archive.
.B \-\-restore \-\-delta
targets only the drift
.B \-\-audit
reports \(em the
.BR mutated ,
.B pruned
and
.B renamed
buckets \(em repairing in place rather than rewriting everything.
.PP
Safety is
.B content-first,
because the archive is a point-in-time copy and a live file can legitimately be
.I ahead
of it. Restore never overwrites an
.B identical
live file (nothing to do), and never overwrites a live file the archived copy is
a strict
.B prefix
of \(em the
.B grown
bucket, an in-flight session that has appended since the last archive run.
Overwriting that would drop the live tail the archive never held, so
.B grown is not a restore target
\(em enforced by comparing bytes, not by trusting a modification time.
.PP
A live file that has diverged some
.I other
way (rewritten or truncated) but carries a
.B newer
modification time than the archived copy may be a fresh in-flight rewrite.
Restore
.B refuses
it \(em loudly, with a non-zero exit \(em rather than overwriting silently;
.B \-\-force
is the deliberate override. Targets with no live file at the restore path (the
.B pruned
and
.B renamed
cases) cannot clobber anything and are always written.
.PP
The
.B renamed
bucket restores the
.I old,
archived path. The archive is keyed by the path the transcript had when it was
captured; a rename left that path empty in the live store and moved the content
to a new one. Restore re-materialises the archived (old) path and leaves the
live renamed copy at its new path untouched \(em the content is byte-identical,
so this adds a copy under the old name rather than moving anything, and never
destroys the live rename. A restored file is stamped with the archived copy's
modification
time, so a following
.B \-\-audit
reads it as
.B synced
and a repeated restore is idempotent.
.PP
Restore checks before it writes. The gunzipped bytes of each target are compared
with the sha256 the manifest recorded, and a copy that does not match (bit rot,
tampering) is
.B refused
and reported, not written; even
.B \-\-force
does not override that, because the bytes are provably not what was captured. An
entry with no recorded sha256 cannot be checked, so it is refused as
.B unverified
unless
.B \-\-force
is given, in which case it is restored and marked unverified in the report. A
mirror that will not decompress is reported by name and the restore carries on.
.B \-\-dry\-run
previews the plan and writes nothing. Restore writes only under the source tree;
a manifest key that would resolve outside it is refused.
.PP
Unlike
.B \-\-verify
and
.BR \-\-audit ,
.B \-\-restore
does
.I not
skip an evicted
.RB ( dataless )
archive file: restoring means getting its content back, so faulting it down from
iCloud is exactly the intended read, not a gratuitous one. See
.BR DATALESS .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.claude/projects/
The source: Claude Code's raw session logs and the sidecars and memory beside
them (read-only to this tool). See
.B CAPTURE
for exactly which files are taken.
.TP
.I ~/.claude/history.jsonl
The top-level prompt/command history, one level above the source root, also
captured (read-only). Relocate with
.BR CCARCHIVE_HISTORY .
.TP
.I <dest>/<rel>/<name>.gz
An archived file \(em the gzip of one captured source file (a transcript, a
subagent
.IR .meta.json ,
a tool-result sidecar or a memory
.IR .md ).
.TP
.I <dest>/_external/history.jsonl.gz
The archived top-level history, under a reserved
.I _external/
path that cannot collide with an encoded project directory.
.TP
.I <dest>/manifest.json
The integrity manifest: one sha256 (plus size and archive time) per archived
file, of every captured class alike.
.TP
.I <dest>/manifest.json.sig
The detached manifest signature (HMAC-SHA256 over the manifest bytes). See
.BR SIGNING .
.TP
.I ~/.claude/ccarchive-signing.key
The signing key (hex, mode 0600), off the archive volume. Override with
.BR CCARCHIVE_KEYFILE .
Back it up out-of-band \(em a new machine needs it to verify.
.TP
.I <dest>/.<name>.<pid>.<random>.tmp
A temporary file of a write in progress, or left by a run that died. See
.BR "ATOMIC WRITES" .
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B ccarchive
Mirror new or changed sessions into the archive.
.TP
.B ccarchive \-\-dry\-run
Preview what a run would archive without writing anything.
.TP
.B ccarchive \-\-verify
Confirm every archived transcript still matches its recorded sha256 and the
manifest's signature is intact \(em run this after restoring the archive to a
new machine (bring the signing key too, or the signature reads as unverifiable).
.TP
.B ccarchive \-\-rekey
Roll the signing key and re-sign the manifest \(em after suspected key exposure,
or on a cadence.
.TP
.B ccarchive \-\-audit
Report whether any live transcript has drifted (been rewritten, truncated or
moved) from what the archive preserved.
.TP
.B ccarchive \-\-restore \-\-delta \-\-dry\-run
Preview the transcripts a delta restore would replace, writing nothing.
.TP
.B ccarchive \-\-restore
Rebuild the live store from the archive \(em restore every archived transcript,
skipping any that already match or that the live store is ahead of.
.TP
.B ccarchive \-\-dest ~/backup/cc
Archive somewhere other than iCloud Drive.
.TP
.B ccarchive \-\-json | jq .archived
How many sessions were archived on this run.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B 0
on success (including a run or verify with nothing to do).
.B 1
means the command finished and found something that needs a person.
.B 2
means it could not finish: one clean line on standard error, or a structured
error object under
.BR \-\-json ,
and no stack trace.
.PP
An ordinary run exits
.B 1
when it refuses a shrink overwrite (a source smaller than the size recorded at
capture;
.B \-\-force
overrides), when it refuses an archive destination inside a git work tree
.RB ( \-\-allow\-repo\-dest
overrides), when the layout-drift alarm fires (zero transcripts found while the
manifest is non-empty), or \(em after completing everything else \(em when it
skipped a source it could not read, refused to write a mirror through a symbolic
link inside the archive, or found an archived mirror that will not decompress.
See
.BR INTEGRITY ,
.B ATOMIC WRITES
and
.BR "ONE BAD FILE" .
.B \-\-verify
exits
.B 1
when the archive does not verify: any
.BR mismatch ,
.BR missing ,
.B corrupt
or
.B UNMANIFESTED
file, or a manifest signature that is not
.B verified
\(em a
.BR MISMATCH ,
a different-key signature, an
.B unsigned
manifest, or an
.B unverifiable
one (no key). See
.BR SIGNING .
.B \-\-audit
exits
.B 1
when the live store has drifted: any
.B mutated
or
.B renamed
transcript, or a live file it could not read.
Growth, new files and pruning are expected and exit
.BR 0 .
An
.B evicted
file (dataless, skipped by
.B \-\-verify
or left undetermined by
.BR \-\-audit ,
or a mirror a run did not check) is
.I not
a failure and exits
.B 0
\(em its bytes are intact in the cloud. See
.BR DATALESS .
.B \-\-restore
exits
.B 1
when it refuses to overwrite a live file newer than the archived copy
.RB ( \-\-force
overrides), when it refuses an archived copy that fails its recorded sha256 or
has
none, or when a target cannot be restored (a manifest entry whose
.I .gz
is gone or will not decompress, or a file it could not read). A restore that
only
writes, skips identical or ahead files, or has nothing to do exits
.BR 0 .
.PP
.B 2
is also what a removed schedule flag, an unreadable or invalid manifest in a
read-only mode, and an I/O failure that stops a run (for example a destination
that cannot be written) exit with.
.SH NOTES
The default destination is the operator's iCloud Drive, derived at runtime
from the home directory; no personal path is baked into the tool. It reads the
source read-only and writes only under the destination, plus the signing key at
.I ~/.claude/ccarchive-signing.key
.RB ( CCARCHIVE_KEYFILE
to relocate). Under
.BR \-\-restore ,
it writes live files under the source tree. A destination inside a git work tree
is refused, and the check resolves symbolic links, so a destination that is a
link into a work tree is refused too.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cctranscript (1),
.BR ccrepo (1),
.BR gzip (1),
.BR zgrep (1)
.PP
